From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 09:59:59 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: wire the RCU software reset as restart handler

Asking this SoC to reboot prints "reboot: Restarting system" and then
stops there forever. Nothing in the tree registers a restart handler, so
machine_restart() finds _machine_restart NULL after do_kernel_restart()
walks an empty notifier chain, and drops into machine_hang(), which
masks every interrupt and parks the CPU in wait. The only way out is the
mains switch. On a board whose sole debug channel is a serial cable that
is already tiresome by hand, and it is fatal to anything that has to
reboot unattended -- a firmware upgrade being the obvious one.

The reset the vendor firmware uses is a single bit. AVM's 4.9 BSP
registers intel_reset_restart_handler() out of
drivers/reset/reset-intel-syscon.c, and the whole handler is one call
asserting the line it numbers RCU_RD_SRST == 30: a read-modify-write
setting bit 30 of RCU_RST_REQ, offset 0x10 into the RCU block, physical
0x16000010. That bit is the SoC's global software reset.

Reaching it costs nothing new here, because the RCU is already described
as "simple-mfd"/"syscon" -- reset-lantiq.c needs it that way to hand out
the GPHY reset lines. When a syscon-reboot node carries no "regmap"
phandle the driver falls back to syscon_node_to_regmap() on its parent,
which is precisely that regmap, so a child node with a compatible, an
offset and a bit is the entire binding; no reg property is involved.
bcm63268.dtsi describes its reset the same way, as a child of the
pll_cntl syscon.

Both mask and value are stated on purpose. syscon-reboot still honours
an older binding in which a mask with no value means "write this word",
so a mask-only node would clear every other bit of RCU_RST_REQ on its
way to setting one. Giving the pair selects regmap_update_bits() and
reproduces the vendor sequence exactly.

Global SRST is also the right scope, rather than resetting only the
cluster Linux runs on. The GRX350 has two interAptiv clusters, and on
the FRITZ!Box 7560 the second one is executing AVM's bootcore from its
own kernel image for as long as the board is powered. Bit 30 resets the
chip, both clusters included, which is what the vendor firmware does on
every reboot and what leaves the bootcore restarted from its entry point
instead of running on underneath a freshly started Linux.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
index c311c2ed7595..8426bfbed168 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
@@ -72,6 +72,19 @@ rcu_reset0: reset-controller@10 {
 			reg = <0x10 0x4>, <0x14 0x4>;
 			#reset-cells = <2>;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Restart handler. Bit 30 of RCU_RST_REQ triggers the SoC
+		 * reset, and the write must be a read-modify-write under the
+		 * mask: a legacy full-word write clears reset lines that are
+		 * latched elsewhere in the same register.
+		 */
+		reboot {
+			compatible = "syscon-reboot";
+			offset = <0x10>;	/* RCU_RST_REQ */
+			mask = <0x40000000>;	/* RCU_RD_SRST */
+			value = <0x40000000>;
+		};
 	};
 
 	asc0: serial@16600000 {
